I wanted to know if my understanding of the PexObserve and Assert usage in the test method is correct. Specifically, does the PexObserve object and Assert object in C# use the same mechanism to be displayed in the Test Explorer or is there any distinction between them?

Does PexObserve's "ValueForViewing" method enable me to add any additional data to the "watched" variables to be displayed in the test execution results in Test Explorer? And Assert's AreSame and AreEqual methods are used to make test assertions, so does the usage of these methods in my code affect the results displayed in Test Explorer?


A: PexObserve is not intended to display additional data; its main purpose is to provide the actual values being examined by the test in order for you or Pex to see them in test run results. So the use of PexObserve with "ValueForViewing" is to make those values visually apparent in the "Watch" window during test runs or their examination in the test results in Visual Studio's Test Explorer.

On the other hand, the usage of Assert methods (like AreSame, AreEqual) does affect the results displayed in Test Explorer. Assert methods are used to make assertions about the expected behavior of the code under test. If an assertion fails, that means the expected outcome did not occur — a clear sign that there might be an issue with the tested code. Test results in Test Explorer will indicate whether all assertions passed or if any failed.

So, to summarize, PexObserve lets you examine the values of variables during a test, but it doesn't add data to the Assert results. Assert, on the other hand, both examines the expected outcome of the tested code and adds data to the test results in Test Explorer based on whether those expectations were met or not.

Please let me know if you have any other questions or need further clarification.

A: PexObserve's "ValueForViewing" method in C# does not provide the capability to add any additional data to be displayed in the test execution results in the Test Explorer. It's main purpose is to display the actual values of variables. It's used in conjunction with PexInline on methods to display input values in the Visual Studio Test Explorer.

As for the Assert methods, "AreSame" and "AreEqual", they do affect the results in Test Explorer. Assertions are used to make test assertions or expectations about the expected behavior of the code under test. If an assertion fails, it indicates there is an issue with the tested code. The test results in Test Explorer will indicate whether all assertions have passed or if any failed. Assert methods provide a way to validate the expected outcome and add data to the test results. 

To summarize: While the PexObserve's "ValueForViewing" method does allow you to watch values in Visual Studio's Test Explorer, it does not provide any additional data to the Assert methods. And Assert methods can add data to the Test Explorer by validating expected behavior and failing if the outcome deviates from expectations.

