If the return value is negative, the new list does not contain x, otherwise it returns the equivalent of `Old_arrListLastIndexOfX`.

The `PUT_IndexOfContract` method is a Pex test method for the ArrayList class's `IndexOf()` method. The method assumes a precondition of the `IndexOf()` method returning a proper index value (i.e. >= 0 if the item is found, otherwise -1 if the item is not in the list). It prepares a test by setting up the necessary state (such as a populated ArrayList), and then calls the `IndexOf()` method with an item to find. It checks the post-conditions to make sure that the ArrayList has not been modified (in terms of its state and items) by the `IndexOf()` method call.

The `PUT_LastIndexOfContract` method is designed similarly, but instead of `IndexOf()`, it tests the `LastIndexOf()` method.

Note, Pex will automatically generate pre-conditions, post-conditions, and test cases that are consistent with the contract of each method. Pex will create test cases so that the pre-conditions hold when the method is called and the post-conditions will hold when the method returns. For example, if the IndexOf() method contract precondition is that it should not alter the Count of the original ArrayList, then Pex will generate a test case where the pre-condition holds and the post-condition also holds, and another test case where the pre-condition holds and the post-condition does not hold (i.e. the method modifies the Count of the original ArrayList).

Please be aware, Pex uses reflection to identify public methods and parameters and cannot handle methods or parameters that are internal or private.

Test Cases

Pex generates several test cases per method based on its contracts. These tests check to make sure the method behaves correctly and does not change its state to an invalid state.




